      box-shadow: 0 0 5px rgba(0,0,0,0.05);}</style><div class="post-html"><p>Are you ready to move your blog from <strong>Blogger</strong> to <strong>WordPress</strong>? Great choice! WordPress gives you more flexibility, plugins, and professional options for growing your blog.</p>

  <p>In this quick guide, I’ll show you <strong>exactly how to export your Blogger posts and import them into WordPress</strong> — step by step. Let’s get started!</p>

  <div class="section">

    <h2>✅ Step 1: Export Your Blogger Content</h2>

    <ul>

      <li><span class="check">1️⃣</span><strong>Log in to your Blogger Dashboard</strong><br> Go to <a href="https://www.blogger.com/" target="_blank">Blogger.com</a> and sign in with your Google account.</li>

      <li><span class="check">2️⃣</span><strong>Open <code>Settings</code></strong><br> In the left sidebar, click <strong>Settings</strong>.</li>

      <li><span class="check">3️⃣</span><strong>Find “Manage Blog”</strong><br> Scroll down to the <strong>“Manage Blog”</strong> section and click <strong>“Back up content.”</strong></li>

      <li><span class="check">4️⃣</span><strong>Download Your XML File</strong><br> Click the <strong>Download</strong> button. This saves an <strong>XML file</strong> with all your posts, pages, and comments.</li>

    </ul>

  </div>

  <div class="section">

    <h2>✅ Step 2: Import Into WordPress</h2>

    <ul>

      <li><span class="check">1️⃣</span><strong>Log in to Your WordPress Dashboard</strong><br> Make sure your WordPress site is installed and ready to go. Log in to your dashboard.</li>

      <li><span class="check">2️⃣</span><strong>Go to <code>Tools &gt; Import</code></strong><br> In the left sidebar, click <strong>Tools</strong> &gt; <strong>Import</strong>.</li>

      <li><span class="check">3️⃣</span><strong>Install the Blogger Importer</strong><br> Find <strong>Blogger</strong> in the import list. Click <strong>Install Now</strong>, then click <strong>Run Importer</strong>.</li>

      <li><span class="check">4️⃣</span><strong>Upload Your XML File</strong><br> Click <strong>Choose File</strong>, select the XML file you downloaded, then click <strong>Upload file and import</strong>.</li>

      <li><span class="check">5️⃣</span><strong>Assign Authors</strong><br> WordPress will ask you to match your Blogger authors to WordPress users — pick an existing user or create a new one.</li>

      <li><span class="check">6️⃣</span><strong>Click Submit</strong><br> WordPress will import all your posts, comments, and categories.</li>

    </ul>

  </div>

  <div class="section">

    <h2>✅ Step 3: Review Your Imported Content</h2>

    <p>Once the import is done:</p>

    <ul>

      <li>Check <strong>Posts &gt; All Posts</strong> and <strong>Pages</strong>.</li>

      <li>Make sure formatting looks good.</li>

      <li>Re-upload images if any didn’t come over correctly.</li>

    </ul>

  </div>

  <div class="section">

    <h2>✅ Optional: Redirect Blogger Visitors to WordPress</h2>

    <p>Don’t lose your SEO! Set up a redirect so visitors to your old Blogger posts go straight to your new WordPress blog.</p>

    <p>Use a plugin like <strong>Blogger to WordPress Redirection</strong> (for WordPress.org sites). It will generate code for your old Blogger theme, so visitors and search engines know your blog has moved.</p>

  </div>

  <h2>🎉 You Did It!</h2>

  <p>And that’s it — you’ve successfully moved your blog from <strong>Blogger</strong> to <strong>WordPress</strong>! Enjoy your new site’s freedom and powerful features.</p>

  <p>If you’d like help picking plugins, choosing a theme, or boosting your SEO on WordPress, drop a comment below — I’m here to help! 🚀✨</p></div>','published','2025-07-12T12:06:00.003Z',null,'📤 How to Export Posts from Blogger to WordPress (Step-by-Step Guide)','How to Export Posts from Blogger to WordPress','How to Export Posts from Blogger to WordPress',array['tech']::text[],'ELIAH TECH',(select id from public.categories where slug='tech-tips-tutorials'),null,'guide',null,null,null,null,2,false,false,false) on conflict(slug) do nothing;
